<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Montenegro</h1><p class="st-meta">2006 CE – present · capital Podgorica</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Present-day Montenegro, independent again since 2006 after nearly nine decades within Yugoslav states, and a NATO member seeking to join the EU.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milo-dukanovic">Milo Đukanović</a>, Montenegro distanced itself from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> in the late 1990s and adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutsche-mark">Deutsche Mark</a> and later the euro. Within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia-and-montenegro">Serbia and Montenegro</a>, it used its right to hold an independence referendum, and on 21 May 2006 a narrow majority voted to leave. Independence was declared on 3 June 2006, restoring the statehood lost in 1918 by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-montenegro">Kingdom of Montenegro</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Montenegro is a parliamentary republic with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica">Podgorica</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a>, the old royal capital, keeps the official residence of the president. A constitution adopted in 2007 made Montenegrin the official language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milo-dukanovic">Đukanović</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-of-socialists-of-montenegro">Democratic Party of Socialists</a> dominated politics for three decades, and tourism along the Adriatic coast is the mainstay of the economy.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Montenegro joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 2017 despite Russian opposition and an alleged coup plot in 2016. A dispute over a law on church property brought the opposition to power in 2020, ending thirty years of rule by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-of-socialists-of-montenegro">Democratic Party of Socialists</a>. It is among the most advanced candidates for EU membership.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/montenegro.jpg" alt="Montenegro" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: B1mbo, Froztbyte, Great Brightstar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Montenegro.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2006 CE – present</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Podgorica</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Milo Đukanović, Filip Vujanović, Jakov Milatović</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Montenegrin, Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Crna Gora</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2016&amp;focus=montenegro" aria-label="Montenegro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5147 668 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Montenegro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5467 924L5488 850"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5467 924h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5467 924h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Podgorica. <a href="/atlas?year=2016&amp;focus=montenegro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
